::ALG2_U02_L07_GP01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup> - 7x + 10 = 0. {
= x = 2 and x = 5#Correct. The expression factors as (x - 2)(x - 5).
~ x = -2 and x = -5#These signs do not make the factors equal 0.
~ x = 7 and x = 10#These are coefficients, not solutions.
~ x = 0 and x = 10#The constant is not automatically a zero.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_GP02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which interval solves x<sup>2</sup> - 9 &lt; 0? {
~ x &lt; -3 or x &gt; 3#That is where the graph is above the x-axis.
= -3 &lt; x &lt; 3#Correct. The parabola is below the x-axis between its zeros.
~ x <= -3 or x >= 3#Those intervals include and extend outside the zeros.
~ all real numbers#The quadratic is not negative for every input.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_GP03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>For y <= x<sup>2</sup> + 2, how should the boundary be drawn? {
~ Dashed, because the inequality includes equality#Including equality means the boundary is solid, not dashed.
~ Dashed, because the graph is a parabola#Boundary style depends on equality, not on being a parabola.
= Solid, because points on the parabola are included#Correct. The symbol <= includes equality.
~ No boundary is drawn#A two-variable inequality needs a boundary graph.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_GP04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>For y > -x<sup>2</sup> + 4, where is the solution region? {
~ Below the parabola#The symbol > means y-values greater than the boundary.
~ Only on the parabola#The strict inequality excludes boundary points.
~ Outside the zeros only#This describes a one-variable interval, not a region.
= Above the parabola#Correct. y is greater than the boundary expression.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_GP05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>A quadratic has zeros -4 and 1 and opens upward. Where is f(x) <= 0? {
~ x < -4 or x > 1#That is where an upward-opening graph is above the x-axis.
= -4 <= x <= 1#Correct. The graph is on or below the x-axis between the zeros.
~ x <= -4 only#This ignores the interval between the zeros.
~ all real numbers#The graph is positive outside the zeros.
}

